WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.370
An emergency room does not treat patients

2
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:06.870
in the order they walk in.

3
00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:08.790
A broken arm waits.

4
00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.180
A heart attack goes first.

5
00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.420
Whenever a doctor is free

6
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:15.128
the question is always the same

7
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.113
who is the most urgent person

8
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.810
waiting right now?

9
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:21.650
Computers ask this all the time.

10
00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.130
Which task should the processor run next?

11
00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.254
Which flight should be boarded first?

12
00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:28.329
Which road should a route

13
00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.120
planner explore next?

14
00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.481
We need a collection where putting

15
00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:33.800
something in is fast

16
00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:35.834
and taking out the most urgent

17
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.540
item is fast too.

18
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:40.510
That collection is a priority queue.

19
00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.010
This is the first video of the chapter

20
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.410
on heaps and priority queues.

21
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.950
We'll see what a priority queue promises

22
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:50.870
two simple ways to build one

23
00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:52.960
and why neither is good enough.

24
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:56.210
A priority queue stores entries.

25
00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.950
Each entry is a pair: a key

26
00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.630
which is the priority, and a value

27
00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:03.010
which is the thing we care about

28
00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:05.020
like a patient or a task.

29
00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.680
By convention

30
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.820
the smaller key is the more urgent one.

31
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.730
Key one beats key three.

32
00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.330
There are only a few operations.

33
00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.702
Insert puts in a new entry.

34
00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:17.044
Min looks at the entry with

35
00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.760
the smallest key, without removing it.

36
00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.508
Remove min takes that entry

37
00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.240
out and returns it.

38
00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:27.500
And there is a size, and a test for empty.

39
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.670
That is all.

40
00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.138
Notice what is missing.

41
00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.846
There is no operation for "give

42
00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:35.387
me the third smallest"

43
00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.174
and no promise that the entries

44
00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.190
are kept in sorted order.

45
00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:42.500
The only promise is about the smallest one.

46
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.850
The simplest idea

47
00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.760
keep the entries in a list, in any order.

48
00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:49.880
Insert is trivial.

49
00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.060
Put the new entry at the end.

50
00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.080
One step

51
00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.310
no matter how many entries there are.

52
00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.590
But to find the smallest

53
00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.350
we have to look at every entry

54
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.111
because it could be anywhere.

55
00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:02.696
Remove min takes a number of

56
00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:04.820
steps proportional to n.

57
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.490
Second idea: keep the list sorted

58
00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.440
with the smallest at the end.

59
00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:13.120
Now remove min is trivial.

60
00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:14.500
Take the last entry.

61
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.670
One step.

62
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.710
But insert has to find the right place

63
00:02:18.710 --> 00:02:22.250
and then make room, shifting entries over.

64
00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:25.020
That is up to n steps.

65
00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.610
So we have not removed the cost.

66
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.592
We have moved it from taking

67
00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:31.370
out to putting in.

68
00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.300
Pause and predict.

69
00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.480
We insert a thousand entries

70
00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.620
one after another

71
00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.670
and then remove all of them, smallest first.

72
00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.390
How many steps does the unsorted list need?

73
00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:44.980
And the sorted list?

74
00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.084
The unsorted list needs

75
00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.640
half a million steps.

76
00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.420
And the sorted list?

77
00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.860
Also half a million, in the worst case

78
00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.370
when the keys arrive in increasing order.

79
00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.624
Neither list is cheap.

80
00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.347
Sorting the collection did

81
00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:01.030
not make the job easier.

82
00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.680
It only changed who pays.

83
00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:07.490
There is a third structure, called a heap

84
00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.220
and it makes a different bargain.

85
00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:11.700
A heap is not sorted.

86
00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.940
It only keeps the smallest entry at the top

87
00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.810
and a loose order below it.

88
00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.670
That is exactly the promise we need

89
00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:20.824
and it is cheap to keep.

90
00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.196
Insert and remove min both cost

91
00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:25.240
about the logarithm of n.

92
00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.120
For a thousand entries

93
00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.300
that is about ten steps, not a thousand.

94
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.590
We will build it in the next video.

95
00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:35.960
Let's count, with the same job as before

96
00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:39.300
insert n entries, then remove all of them.

97
00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.320
Increasing keys

98
00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.970
so the sorted list is at its worst.

99
00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.195
For five hundred entries

100
00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.832
each list needs about one hundred

101
00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:49.395
twenty-five thousand steps.

102
00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:51.703
The heap needs about seven

103
00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:53.280
thousand comparisons.

104
00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:55.260
For four thousand entries

105
00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.655
each list needs eight million steps.

106
00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.456
The heap needs about eighty

107
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:00.880
thousand comparisons.

108
00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.750
A hundred times less.

109
00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.270
A step is not exactly a comparison

110
00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.870
so don't read this as a precise ratio.

111
00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:09.490
But the shape is clear

112
00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:11.850
one grows with n squared

113
00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.900
the other with n log n.

114
00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:15.690
To sum up.

115
00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.689
A priority queue promises one thing

116
00:04:18.770 --> 00:04:20.991
remove min returns the entry

117
00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:22.760
with the smallest key.

118
00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:26.726
A sorted list and an unsorted list both pay about

119
00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:29.500
n steps for one of the two operations.

120
00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:32.710
A heap pays about log n for both.

121
00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.190
Next time

122
00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.486
how a heap is laid out in an array

123
00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:38.445
and how up-heap and down-heap

124
00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:39.660
keep it in shape.

125
00:04:40.287 --> 00:04:41.922
Let's build the two list-based

126
00:04:41.977 --> 00:04:43.357
priority queues in Java

127
00:04:43.357 --> 00:04:45.327
and measure them against a heap.

128
00:04:45.907 --> 00:04:49.085
An entry is a record with a key and a value.

129
00:04:49.157 --> 00:04:51.976
The heap-based queue wraps them and

130
00:04:52.057 --> 00:04:54.027
takes a comparator for the keys.

131
00:04:54.377 --> 00:04:56.487
So the keys can be any type

132
00:04:56.487 --> 00:04:59.247
and you decide what small means.

133
00:04:59.247 --> 00:05:01.217
The values are never compared.

134
00:05:01.797 --> 00:05:03.414
In the unsorted queue

135
00:05:03.487 --> 00:05:05.061
insert is one add at the

136
00:05:05.127 --> 00:05:06.827
end of an array list.

137
00:05:06.827 --> 00:05:09.707
Remove min loops over every entry

138
00:05:09.707 --> 00:05:11.607
remembering the index of the smallest

139
00:05:11.607 --> 00:05:12.757
then removes it.

140
00:05:13.107 --> 00:05:16.827
The field steps counts each entry looked at.

141
00:05:17.407 --> 00:05:19.122
The sorted queue keeps its list

142
00:05:19.177 --> 00:05:20.886
in decreasing key order

143
00:05:20.957 --> 00:05:23.611
so the smallest is at the end and remove

144
00:05:23.677 --> 00:05:26.527
min is a single remove at the end.

145
00:05:26.877 --> 00:05:29.259
Insert starts at the end and shifts

146
00:05:29.327 --> 00:05:31.468
entries with a smaller key one place

147
00:05:31.527 --> 00:05:33.957
over to make room, counting steps.

148
00:05:34.537 --> 00:05:36.227
Here are the real counts

149
00:05:36.227 --> 00:05:39.338
for n entries inserted and then all removed.

150
00:05:39.407 --> 00:05:42.908
With increasing keys both lists need exactly

151
00:05:42.987 --> 00:05:47.147
n plus n times n minus one over two steps.

152
00:05:47.147 --> 00:05:49.767
A thousand entries: five hundred thousand

153
00:05:49.767 --> 00:05:50.577
five hundred.

154
00:05:50.927 --> 00:05:53.677
The heap-based queue, on the same job

155
00:05:53.677 --> 00:05:56.927
needs about sixteen thousand comparisons.

156
00:05:57.277 --> 00:05:59.312
With random keys the sorted

157
00:05:59.387 --> 00:06:00.486
list gets cheaper

158
00:06:00.547 --> 00:06:02.412
about a quarter of a million steps

159
00:06:02.467 --> 00:06:03.770
for a thousand entries

160
00:06:03.827 --> 00:06:05.987
because inserts shift fewer

161
00:06:06.067 --> 00:06:07.287
entries on average.

162
00:06:07.287 --> 00:06:09.254
But it is still quadratic.

163
00:06:09.327 --> 00:06:11.531
The unsorted list still needs

164
00:06:11.607 --> 00:06:12.984
five hundred thousand

165
00:06:13.047 --> 00:06:15.075
and the heap about seventeen

166
00:06:15.147 --> 00:06:16.757
thousand comparisons.

167
00:06:17.337 --> 00:06:20.107
Java has a heap in the standard library

168
00:06:20.107 --> 00:06:22.547
the Priority Queue class.

169
00:06:22.547 --> 00:06:24.527
Offer adds an item.

170
00:06:24.527 --> 00:06:27.627
Poll removes and returns the smallest.

171
00:06:27.627 --> 00:06:28.777
Peek looks at it.

172
00:06:29.127 --> 00:06:31.074
Print it after seven offers and

173
00:06:31.137 --> 00:06:35.217
you see the array inside: one, three, two

174
00:06:35.217 --> 00:06:37.137
five, four, nine, eight.

175
00:06:37.137 --> 00:06:38.247
Not sorted.

176
00:06:38.597 --> 00:06:40.747
That is also a trap.

177
00:06:40.747 --> 00:06:42.647
Iterating over a Priority Queue

178
00:06:42.647 --> 00:06:45.547
or printing it, shows the array order

179
00:06:45.547 --> 00:06:47.447
not the priority order.

180
00:06:47.447 --> 00:06:50.237
Only poll gives you the entries in order.

181
00:06:50.817 --> 00:06:53.117
To attach a value, make a record

182
00:06:53.187 --> 00:06:55.250
and give the queue a comparator that

183
00:06:55.307 --> 00:06:57.137
looks only at the priority.

184
00:06:57.487 --> 00:06:58.857
For a max-heap

185
00:06:58.857 --> 00:07:01.127
pass the reverse order comparator.

186
00:07:01.477 --> 00:07:03.507
Null is not allowed

187
00:07:03.507 --> 00:07:06.717
offering null throws a NullPointerException.

188
00:07:07.297 --> 00:07:09.828
Equal priorities do not come out

189
00:07:09.907 --> 00:07:11.367
in the order they went in.

190
00:07:11.367 --> 00:07:16.907
Here, a, b, c, d, e came out as a, e, d, c

191
00:07:16.907 --> 00:07:17.417
b.

192
00:07:17.767 --> 00:07:20.197
If order among equals matters

193
00:07:20.197 --> 00:07:22.687
add a sequence number to the comparator.

194
00:07:23.037 --> 00:07:25.527
And there is no decrease key.

195
00:07:25.527 --> 00:07:27.687
Removing an arbitrary item with remove

196
00:07:27.687 --> 00:07:29.667
or checking with contains

197
00:07:29.667 --> 00:07:33.037
scans the whole queue, so it costs n.

198
00:07:33.617 --> 00:07:35.167
So in Java

199
00:07:35.167 --> 00:07:37.127
use Priority Queue with a comparator

200
00:07:37.127 --> 00:07:40.007
remember that printing shows array order

201
00:07:40.007 --> 00:07:41.367
break ties yourself

202
00:07:41.367 --> 00:07:44.017
and expect no decrease key.

203
00:07:44.367 --> 00:07:47.367
Next: how the heap works inside.

